WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.350
- [Narrator] If you're
looking for a job in New York

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.570
starting May 15th

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.830
you'll be able to see
what positions will pay

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.910
on a whole lot more job postings.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:10.850
This is thanks to a new
law that passed in January

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.350
requiring nearly all New
York City companies to

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:17.100
disclose expected salary
ranges for all open positions.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.910
- This is a big deal because of the size

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.320
of New York city's economy.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.770
Many of the biggest companies
have operations in New York.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.240
- [Narrator] Pay transparency exists

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.000
to some degree in several places

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.300
in the US, but New York City
would join only Colorado

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.900
in requiring this information
be included in job postings.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.260
- I think it'll make a difference

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.150
in a really positive way for job seekers

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.375
especially women and black
people and people of color

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:43.850
- But some businesses have voiced concern

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.010
that it will cause more harm than good.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.356
So what does this new
law mean for job seekers

00:00:48.356 --> 00:00:50.460
employees and companies?

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.780
How will it impact the
city's labor market?

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.750
And will it be an effective
way to fight pay inequities?

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.640
New York City's pay transparency law

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.420
was introduced in 2018

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.912
but no action was taken
until last December.

00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:05.980
- It comes at a time when the
labor market is really tight.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.600
And when the power
dynamics have already sort

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.590
of shifted to employees.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.850
- [Narrator] The law covers nearly

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.780
every employer hiring in the city

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.550
except those with fewer
than four employees.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.040
And if companies don't comply

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.961
they could be fined up to $250,000

00:01:19.961 --> 00:01:22.480
or face other civil penalties.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.520
- New York City officials have told me

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.940
that basically at first they plan to

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.000
not focus as much on fines

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.833
but on just making sure

00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:32.036
that people comply and
understand this rule.

00:01:32.036 --> 00:01:33.140
- [Narrator] While the commission

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.960
on human rights will do some
investigating on its own

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.840
most of the time it will
act following a complaint.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.580
This mandate joins another law

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.640
in New York that passed in 2017

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.580
which banned private employers
from pressing job seekers

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.920
to disclose their salary history.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.330
The first group that will benefit

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.970
from this new law, job seekers.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:52.889
By seeing pay ranges

00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:56.350
they can be more prepared
going into salary negotiations

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.570
and only apply for jobs that
pay their desired salary.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.110
This rule will also
enable existing employees

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.080
to compare their pay with
those stated in open roles.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.870
- Many employment lawyers and others

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.610
expect existing employees
to go to their bosses

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.020
and say, Hey, I see this
salary range is posted

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.320
why am I not at the
high end of that range.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.242
- [Narrator] But this law could
be particularly significant

00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:18.130
for women and people of color

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.840
two groups who have been
historically underpaid.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.580
According to the US census bureaus'

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.310
most recent national data

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.430
women in the US make 30% less than men.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.453
And that gap is wider
for most women of color.

00:02:30.453 --> 00:02:33.240
Paris Chandler is the Founder and CEO

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.710
of Black Tech Pipeline

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.940
an organization that
helps black technologists

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.330
get jobs at companies across the US.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.020
She says pay transparency
will help prevent

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.840
candidates from underselling themselves

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.220
in the to interview process.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.030
- When you don't know what to ask for

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.300
it brings on a lot a lot of stress

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.040
anxiety and imposter syndrome

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.739
because you don't know
what to value yourself at.

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:57.620
Especially as someone who's
used to being underserved.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.870
- One group that's pushing
back against this law

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.990
is the partnership for New York City

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.920
a prominent business group that represents

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.810
over 300 major companies in New York City.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.750
It intends to push for the
city to delay implementation

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.800
of the law to the end of the year.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.480
Catherine Wylde, the
group's president and CEO

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.400
says the law would be
difficult to implement

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.697
especially with so many remote jobs.

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:21.896
And that in some industries,
salary is only one component

00:03:21.896 --> 00:03:24.470
of an employee's total compensation.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.070
- Often, like in the
financial services industry

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.550
it's a small component
of total compensation

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.893
but you don't really know
what the compensation

00:03:32.893 --> 00:03:35.910
is going to be like
for performance bonuses

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.390
or in the case of the tech startup sector

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.412
the equity opportunities.

00:03:40.412 --> 00:03:42.530
- [Narrator] And in a
competitive labor market

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:43.520
like New York City

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.670
she says this could put
employers at a disadvantage.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.810
- The idea that somehow
you're putting out there

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.940
the maximum you will pay

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:53.290
cuts you off from talent

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.140
and gives your competitors
an opportunity to outbid you.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.220
- She added that the law would
negatively impact hospitals

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:01.420
and small businesses

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.540
which are both experiencing
staffing shortages.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.690
New York City's mandate comes about a year

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.770
after Colorado's pay transparency
law went into effect.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.120
And there, companies have handled
the law in different ways.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.390
Some got around the
disclosure requirements

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.950
by specifying in their listings

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.210
that the work can't be done in the state.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:23.720
Others posted salaries with
ranges of $100,000 or more.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.990
- But overall, the results from Colorado

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:27.537
have been pretty positive

00:04:27.537 --> 00:04:29.900
according to job seekers and others.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:33.530
Most jobs now include
salary information on them.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.410
The state has done a lot to
sort of educate companies

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.290
on how to comply with this.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:39.890
And as a result, you can then look

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.030
at postings from big companies
like Amazon and others

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.939
and see what a software engineering role

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:47.100
in Colorado might pay for example

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.720
what a pharmacist at Walmart
might pay in Colorado.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.600
It gives people a sense for
sort of what the jobs pay there

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.430
even if the pay might be different
in someone else's region.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.760
- [Narrator] A Colorado trade
group sought an injunction

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.770
against the rule in 2021.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:02.160
Though a federal judge denied that request

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.090
employment lawyers say they
expect similar lawsuits

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.300
against New York's measure

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.110
before it goes into effect in May.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.071
Over the next few months

00:05:10.071 --> 00:05:12.580
experts say more details about the law

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:13.940
are expected to come out.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.550
Like how companies will
be expected to comply

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.510
and what the enforcement
mechanism might look like.

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:21.950
- I think many people are
watching New York City's law

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:23.710
just because it could have ripple effects

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.050
for other economies around the country.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.790
Other cities and states may
follow New York City here

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.640
and put in place similar legislation.

